WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.720
The Indian Child Welfare Act turns 40
this year, but it may not live much

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.349
longer. The law passed as an attempt to
ensure that American Indian kids weren't

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:18.300
being taken away from their families on
account of poverty or sheer bigotry. It

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.930
says the preference for adoption of
Indian foster children must be given to

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:26.279
Indians, regardless of tribe, even over
the objection of the children's

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:31.050
biological parents, and even if that
child has formed a years-long bond with

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.940
non-Indian foster parents. It was a well intended law that now protects the

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.570
interests of tribes over those of
children, creating a separate but unequal

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:44.190
child welfare system that leaves Indian
children in foster care far longer than

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.090
children of other races, and keeps
families of all races from adopting

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:52.680
children they have loved and cared for. A
federal judge in Texas recently declared

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.079
the law unconstitutional, a violation of
equal protection and federalism, and the

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:02.789
Supreme Court will likely take it up as
a result. It's about time. All kids

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:07.460
deserve a loving stable home. It's time
to stop treating Indian kids differently.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.840
Do you think American child welfare
policies are fair? Let us know in your

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:15.780
comments. Also, let us know what other
